  "raw_text": "Step 1: Recall the definitions of even and odd functions.\n- A function \\( h(x) \\) is even if \\( h(-x) = h(x) \\) for all \\( x \\) in the domain of \\( h \\).\n- A function \\( h(x) \\) is odd if \\( h(-x) = -h(x) \\) for all \\( x \\) in the domain of \\( h \\).\n\nStep 2: Apply these definitions to \\( f(x) \\) and \\( g(x) \\).\n- Since \\( f(x) \\) is odd, we have \\( f(-x) = -f(x) \\).\n- Since \\( g(x) \\) is even, we have \\( g(-x) = g(x) \\).\n\nStep 3: Analyze the composition \\( f(g(f(g(f(x))))) \\).\n- Start from the innermost function and work outward.\n- Let \\( y = f(g(f(x))) \\). We need to determine if \\( f(g(y)) \\) is even, odd, or neither when \\( y \\) is replaced by \\( -y \\).\n\nStep 4: Substitute \\( -x \\) into the expression.\n- Consider \\( f(g(f(g(f(-x))))) \\).\n- Since \\( f(x) \\) is odd, \\( f(-x) = -f(x) \\), so \\( f(g(f(-x))) = f(g(-f(x))) \\).\n- Since \\( g(x) \\) is even, \\( g(-f(x)) = g(f(x)) \\), thus \\( f(g(f(-x))) = f(g(f(x))) \\).\n- Therefore, \\( f(g(f(g(f(-x))))) = f(g(f(g(f(x))))) \\).\n\nStep 5: Conclude that the entire expression is even.\n- The expression \\( f(g(f(g(f(x))))) \\) remains unchanged when \\( x \\) is replaced by \\( -x \\), indicating it is an even function.\n\nFinal answer: even<|im_end|>",
